course: dictionary
lesson: 6528
title: The ending -ness
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The ending «-ness» is a suffix, a small part added to the end of a word. It turns a describing word (an adjective) into a naming word (a noun) for a quality or state. Kind becomes kindness, the quality of being kind.
explain: Find the root first, then add the ending. Happy + ness = happiness. Dark + ness = darkness. If the word ends in y, change the y to i before adding -ness: lazy becomes laziness. If the root ends in n, keep the n: sudden becomes suddenness.
explain: You can check a hard word by splitting it. Hopelessness is hope + less + ness: hope, without hope, and the state of being without hope. Find the root, then the endings, and the meaning comes together.
explain: Ness can also be a word on its own. A ness is a headland, a piece of high land sticking out into the sea, and it appears in place names like Dungeness and Sheerness. Capital letters show a name: Loch Ness is a long, deep lake in Scotland, famous for «Nessie», the Loch Ness Monster.
fact: The suffix -ness turns an adjective into a noun: kind → kindness, happy → happiness, dark → darkness.
fact: Change a final y to i before -ness: lazy → laziness.
fact: Keep a final n when adding -ness: sudden → suddenness.
fact: Endings that start with a consonant, like -ness, usually do not change the root: careless, useless.
fact: A ness on its own is a headland, high land sticking out into the sea. Loch Ness is a long, deep lake in Scotland.
exercise: What is the root word in «kindness»? | *kind | kin | nice | kindly
exercise: Which word is lazy with -ness added correctly? | *laziness | lazyness | lazieness
exercise: Which word means a headland, high land sticking out into the sea? | loch | *ness | pier | dune
test: What does -ness do to a word like «dark»? | Makes it mean the opposite | Makes it a verb meaning to darken | Makes it plural | *Turns the describing word into a noun for a state, darkness
test: In «Loch Ness is a long, deep lake in Scotland», what is Loch Ness? | *A long, deep lake in Scotland | A kind of monster | A headland in Scotland | A town on the coast
exam: Which is the correct noun made from «happy»? | happines | *happiness | hapiness | happyness
